From 0ed6b6d953a018a585f5fe9fadc64e0d7eca9d46 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: SteveDeFacto <5148995+SteveDeFacto@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 07:35:46 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] Tests: heartbeat placement vs the owner-nomination self-test,
 real Host catalog gate, real catalog version for the Safe startup flow

Test-only repairs of three suites that were reported as baseline failures; no product, policy or trust source changes.

- registry-reconcile: the blanket "no heartbeat send anywhere in supervisor.js" regex could not tell the explicit
  OWNER_NOMINATION_SELFTEST block (a one-shot nonce-concurrency load that exits) from a routine writer. It now asserts
  the placement invariant - every heartbeat send is inside that block, the block always ends in process.exit, routine
  owner nomination never runs under the self-test, and REGISTRY_HEARTBEAT_SEC only names the reconcile cadence - with
  two negative controls: an injected routine heartbeat timer and the REAL pre-change supervisor (9a307f3ea^) are both
  caught. Routine behaviour on a real chain stays test/offchain-heartbeat-writers.test.mjs.
- catalog-platform-enforcement #2: the harness rebuilt walletPolicyRefusal from the source text, which stopped
  compiling once the method moved behind the private #walletPolicyState (the behaviour was intact). It now runs the
  REAL Host over the REAL createHostAdmission with an in-memory chain: forPrivate only for a ledger-private record,
  delisted/yanked/rejected refused for public and private records, the owner's denial, a non-catalog reference refused
  definitely, an unreadable chain UNKNOWN, and the hold channel's decision() path.
- site-independent-account-startup: the Safe-held deployOnChain(prepareOnly) created nothing because the fixture
  deployed catalog://0x11..11/0, an app the rig's catalog does not have, and the browser's catalog moderation gate
  refused it ("could not verify current catalog moderation"). The test now publishes a real version as a third-party
  publisher and proves the refusals (unknown app, pending for a public record, delisted, administrator-rejected; each
  leaves the ledger count unchanged and names its reason) before the administrator approves it and the Safe creates
  the record itself; deployOnChain's return value, error, toasts and run-log lines are captured into its assertions.
